    "reason": "The claim that LLMs acquire biases from training data via statistical correlations is a well-established general fact in machine learning. Training involves minimizing loss over vast datasets where biased patterns (e.g., gender stereotypes, racial prejudices) exist as statistical regularities. The model learns these correlations by adjusting weights to predict the majority pattern found in the data. This mechanism explains how 'inheritance' occurs without requiring explicit instruction of bias. The qualifier 'from training data' correctly scopes the source of this specific inheritance mechanism.",
    "candidate_id": "candidate_0127",
    "position": "defend"
  },
  "candidate_0128": {
    "reason": "The claim that LLMs acquire biases from distillation data via error propagation is a valid general explanatory mechanism. Distillation involves a student model learning to mimic the outputs (logits or probabilities) of a teacher model. If the teacher model contains biases (learned during its own training), these are embedded in its output distributions. The student model, by optimizing to match these outputs, statistically correlates with and propagates those errors. This is distinct from direct training on biased text; it is the propagation of the teacher's learned state. The qualifier 'from distillation data' (referring to the teacher's outputs used as targets) correctly scopes this mechanism.",
    "candidate_id": "candidate_0128",
    "position": "defend"
  }
}
